==== Watch the Seasons of Temptation

#smallcaps[The] other part of our Savior’s direction, namely to “watch,” is more general and extends to many particulars. I will fix on some things contained in it:

1\. Watch the #emph[seasons] in which men usually “enter into temptation.”

There are various seasons in which an hour of temptation is commonly at hand and will unavoidably seize the soul, unless it is delivered by mercy in the use of watchfulness. When we are in such a season, we are to be especially on our guard, so that we do not enter into the power of temptation or fall under it. Some of those seasons may be named:

(1.) A season of #emph[unusual outward prosperity] usually comes with an hour of temptation. Prosperity and temptation go together; indeed, prosperity is a temptation, many temptations. That is because, without outstanding supplies of grace, it tends to put a soul into a frame and temper exposed to any temptation, and it provides fuel and food for them all. It has provision for lust and darts for Satan.

The wise man tells us that “the prosperity of fools will destroy them” (Prov 1:32). It #emph[hardens] them in their way, makes them despise instruction, and makes them put far from them the evil day, whose terror should move them to amend. Without special help it has an inconceivably harmful influence on believers themselves. That is why Agur prays against riches, because of the temptation that comes with them: “Otherwise,” he says, “I may have too much and deny You, saying, ‘Who is the #smallcaps[Lord]?’ ” (Prov 30:8, 9); that is, lest, being filled with them, he should forget the Lord, as God complains his people did (Hos 13:6). We know how David was mistaken in this case: “In prosperity I said, ‘I will never be shaken’ ” (Ps 30:6). All is well, and will be well. But what was at hand, what lay at the door, that David did not think of? “When You hid Your face, I was dismayed” (verse 7). God was ready to hide his face, and David to enter into a temptation of desertion, and he did not know it.

As to a #emph[prosperous] condition, then: I will not run counter to Solomon’s counsel, “In the day of prosperity, be joyful” (Eccl 7:14). Rejoice in the God of your mercies, who does you good in his patience and forbearance, in spite of all your unworthiness. Yet I may add to it, from the same fountain of wisdom, “consider” too, lest evil lie at the door. A man in that state is in the midst of snares. Satan has many advantages against him. He forges darts out of all the man’s enjoyments; and if the man does not watch, he will be entangled before he knows it.

You lack what should steady and ballast your heart. Formality in religion will tend to creep up on you, and that lays the soul open to all temptations in their full power and strength. Satisfaction and delight in the comforts of created things, the poison of the soul, will tend to grow on you. In such a time be vigilant, be careful, or you will be taken by surprise. Job says that in his affliction “God has made my heart soft” (Job 23:16). There is a hardness, a want of spiritual feeling that goes unnoticed, gathered in prosperity. If it is not watched against, it will expose the heart to the deceits of sin and the baits of Satan. “Watch and pray” in this season. Many men’s negligence in it has cost them dearly; their woeful experience cries out to us to take heed. Blessed is the man who fears always, but especially in a time of prosperity.

(2.) As was partly shown before, a time of #emph[the slumber of grace], of neglect in communion with God, of formality in duty, is a season to watch in, as one that certainly has some other temptation coming with it.

Let a soul in such a state wake up and look about him. His enemy is at hand, and he is ready to fall into a condition that may cost him dearly all the days of his life. His present state is bad enough in itself; but it is a sign of something worse that lies at the door. The disciples who were with Christ on the mount had not only a bodily drowsiness on them but a spiritual one. What does our Savior say to them? “Get up, watch and pray so that you#super[+] will not enter into temptation.” We know how near one of them was to a bitter hour of temptation; and not watching as he ought, he immediately entered into it.

I mentioned before the case of the bride (Song 5:2–8). She slept; she was drowsy and unwilling to gird herself for a vigorous performance of duties, in quick, active communion with Christ. Before she knows it, she has lost her beloved. Then she moans, inquires, cries, and endures wounds, reproaches, and everything else before she finds him again. Consider your state and condition, then, O poor soul! Does your light burn dim? Or though it gives as great a blaze to others as before, do you not see the face of God in Christ by it as clearly as you once did (2 Cor 4:6)? Is your zeal cold? Or if it does the same works as before, is your heart not warmed in them, as before, with the love of God and love for God, so that you only go on in the course you have been in? Are you negligent in the duties of praying or hearing? Or if you keep them, do you not do it with the life and vigor you once did? Do you flag in your profession? Or if you keep it up, are your wheels oiled by some crooked motives from within or without? Does your delight in the people of God grow faint and cold? Or is your love for them changing from what is purely spiritual into what is very fleshly, on account of their having principles and natural temperaments that suit yours, if not on worse foundations? If you are drowsing in such a condition as this, beware. You are falling into some woeful temptation that will break all your bones and give you wounds that will stay with you all the days of your life. Indeed, when you wake up, you will find that it has in fact laid hold of you already, though you did not perceive it. It has struck and wounded you, though you have not complained or sought relief or healing.

Such was the state of the church of Sardis (Rev 3:2). “What remains” was “about to die.” “Wake up,” says our Savior, “and strengthen” it, or something worse will befall you. If anyone who reads the word of this direction is in this condition, if he has any regard for his poor soul, let him wake up now, before he is entangled beyond recovery. Take this warning from God; do not despise it.

(3.) A season of #emph[great spiritual enjoyments] is often turned, by the malice of Satan and the weakness of our hearts, into a season of danger in this matter of temptation.

We know how it was with Paul (2 Cor 12:7). He had glorious spiritual revelations of God and Jesus Christ. Immediately Satan falls on him; a messenger of Satan torments him, so that he earnestly begs for it to depart, and yet is left to struggle with it. At times God is pleased to give us special discoveries of himself and his love, to fill the heart with his kindness. Christ takes us into the banqueting house and gives our hearts their fill of love. He does this by some notable work of his Spirit, overpowering us with a sense of love in the unspeakable privilege of adoption, and so fills our souls with joy unspeakable and glorious. A man would think this was the safest condition in the world. What soul does not cry with Peter on the mount, “It is good for us to be here,” to stay here forever? But very often some bitter temptation is at hand just then. Satan sees that, taken up with the joy before us, we quickly neglect many ways of approach to our souls, and in these he seeks and finds advantages against us. Is this, then, our state and condition? Does God at any time give us to drink from the rivers of pleasure at his right hand, and satisfy our souls with his kindness as with marrow and fatness? Let us not say, “We will never be shaken.” We do not know how soon God may hide his face, or a messenger of Satan may torment us.

Besides, a greater and worse deceit often lies in this business. Men cheat their souls with their own fancies instead of a sense of God’s love by the Holy Spirit. And when they are lifted up with their imaginations, it is beyond words how fearfully they are exposed to all kinds of temptations. How, then, can they find relief against their consciences in their own foolish fancies and deceits that they amuse themselves with? Do we not see such people every day, persons walking in the vanities and ways of this world, yet boasting of their sense of the love of God? Shall we believe them? Then we must not believe truth itself. And how woeful their condition must be!

(4.) A fourth season is a season of #emph[self-confidence]\; then temptation is usually at hand.

The case of Peter makes this clear: “I will not deny You. Even if all men deny You, I will not. Even if I were to die for it, I would not do it.” So the poor man spoke when he stood on the very brink of the temptation that cost him such bitter tears in the end. And this taught him to know himself so well all his days, and gave him such an understanding of the state of all believers, that when he had received more of the Spirit and of power, he still had less confidence. He saw it was right for others to have less too, and so he urges all men, “conduct yourselves in reverent fear during your stay as foreigners” (1 Pet 1:17); they are not to be confident and high as he was, lest they fall as he did. At the first trial he compares himself with others and boasts himself above them: “Even if all men forsake You, I will not.” He fears every man more than himself. But when our Savior afterward comes to him and puts the comparison to him directly, “Simon … do you love Me more than these?” (John 21:15), he has done with comparing himself with others, and only cries, “Yes, Lord, You know I love You.” He will lift himself up above others no more. Such a season often comes. Temptations are abroad in the world, false doctrines with countless other allurements and provocations. Every one of us is ready to be very confident that we will not be caught by them. Even if all men fall into these follies, we would not. Surely we will never turn away from our walk with God; it is impossible that our hearts should be so stupid. But the apostle says, “Do not be arrogant, but be afraid”; “the one who thinks he is standing firm should be careful not to fall.” Would you think that Peter, who had walked on the sea with Christ, had confessed him to be the Son of God, and had been with him on the mount when he heard the voice from the majestic glory, would instantly fall to cursing and swearing that he did not know him, at the word of a servant girl, when there was no legal inquiry after him and no proceedings against him or anyone in his condition? Let those who have any mind to beware of sin beware of self-confidence. And this is the first thing in our watching: to consider well the seasons in which temptation usually makes its approaches to the soul, and to be armed against them. And these are some of the seasons in which temptations are near at hand.
